  "worked_solution": "Given an autonomous differential equation ${y}^{\u2032}(t)=f(y),$ if there exists a constant $c$ such that $f(c)=0,$ then $y=c$ is an equilibrium solution of the equation.\n\nSo, to find the equilibrium solutions of a differential equation, we must find all values of $c$ such that $f(c)=0.$\n\nIn our case, we have that $f(y)={y}^{2}-2y-15,$ so we solve the equation $f(c)=0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(c) & =0 \\\\ {c}^{2}-2c-15 & =0 \\\\ (c-5)(c+3) & =0 \\\\ c & =-3,5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, ${y}_{1}(t)=-3$ and ${y}_{2}(t)=5$ are the equilibrium solutions of the given differential equation.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
